{
	title: 'Provider failures fix themselves',
	spec: 'Implement the retrying and blocked states from tasks/j1/states.md (section Failures) with the user decisions there: temporary failures retry at once then back off and never give up on their own; 429 and quota rotate to the next account for the same model (reference: the old account-rotation.ts, artifact) and otherwise wait for the provider-given time, surviving restart; broken auth blocks on login and continues by itself once the credentials file works again (it is a live file); bad requests end the turn in error with the provider message, and continue retries. Laptop sleep must be invisible to the user: this is the one place where defensive, hard-to-test code is allowed. Detect wake with an injected clock (timer gap), treat streams open across it as dropped, and say in comments which failure each guard is for. Every state names its time or reason in snapshots so clients show "retrying in 12s (connection lost)" or "blocked: log in". Quota-limited ongoing turns also watch credential-file changes and ended login attempts, including a login in another tab: recheck accounts within the credential polling interval rather than waiting for the previous reset deadline. Unchanged credentials keep the original deadline; an unsuccessful recheck waits again normally. Idle, paused and ended turns are not resumed by login. Cancel the losing deadline or credential watcher when either finishes, or on pause, model switch or wake.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Two homes sharing one copied credentials file invalidate each other: whichever refreshes first rotates the refresh token and the other gets invalid_grant. Seen on 25 Sep between ~/.hal and Hal. Account rotation and the blocked-on-login state must handle this, not just a truly expired login.',
		"Classification lives in provider.stream: every error event carries `failure` ('temporary' | 'limited' | 'auth', absent = bad request or Hal bug: the turn ends in error) and maybe `retryAt` (epoch ms). Thrown errors keep their `failure`/`retryAt` (auth.ts throws them); network and SSE read errors are temporary, errors from a provider's own request/parse code are not. Messages include the provider's own words (error.message in the body).",
		'host.runTurn retries a failed round inside the same turn (host.waitOut): retrying at retryAt, or at once then 1s, 2s, ... capped at 30s; auth without retryAt blocks and waits on auth.changed() (polls the file stat every 2s, then reopens the live file). Before retrying after partial output it appends a `continue` record, so the model hears it was cut off. Edge: those count toward states.recoveries, so a turn whose stream drops mid-answer 5 times in a row and then loses its host is paused by the loop guard.',
		'Rate limits persist in state/limits.ason (limits.ts), keyed "provider/model" or "provider/model account". A 429 with an account (HttpRequest.account) limits that account (retry-after, anthropic-ratelimit-unified-reset or body resets_at/resets_in_seconds; else provider.accountLimitMs) and retries at once: auth.anthropic(model) skips limited accounts, and throws limited with the earliest reset when all are. Without an account the model itself is limited and provider.stream answers from limits.ason without a request until then; that is what survives restart. Task 61 decides which account goes first (least used, usage.ts).',
		'Broken logins are remembered in memory by a hash of the entry credentials (auth.state.broken), so copying in new credentials ends it by itself. A 401 calls provider.rejected(account): the first per account in 10 minutes marks the token stale (refreshed on next use, retry at once), a second marks the login broken. A refresh 4xx (invalid_grant) is broken; 5xx, 408, 429, network errors and a response with no token are temporary.',
		'Laptop sleep: clock.ts (started by host.init) ticks every second; a tick more than 4s late is a wake. provider.stream aborts its connection on wake and reports temporary "connection lost (the computer slept)"; clock.until waits in 1s slices re-reading wall time and ends on wake. Tests inject clock.now/clock.sleep and call clock.wake()/tick() directly.',
		'states.describe(state, now?) says "retrying in 12s (reason)" given now, else the time of day. Clients still call it without now (no ticking re-render yet), so they show the absolute time.',
		"Bug fixed 7 Oct: OpenAI 429s for a spent 5h window carry no reset in the body, so the one-minute accountLimitMs guess retried every account every minute for hours. Invariant: a 429 without a reset limits the account until its spent (100%) usage window resets, from the same response's headers (usage.observe runs first); accountLimitMs applies only when no window is spent."
	],
